From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 14:09:22 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] nir: index the split 64-bit vec3/vec4 arrays by leaf element

nir_split_64bit_vec3_and_vec4 replaces a function-temp variable whose leaf
type is a dvec3 or dvec4 with a pair of variables that are always a *flat*
array of one vector per leaf element: get_var_pair() sizes them with
glsl_get_aoa_size() times the matrix column count. get_linear_array_offset()
did not index them that way. It took the stride of each array level from
glsl_array_size() of the type that level dereferences, which is only the
length of the next dimension, and fell back to a stride of one whenever that
type was not an array - which is the case for every matrix and for the
innermost vector.

That happens to be right for a one- or two-dimensional array of vectors, and
is wrong for anything deeper and for every array of matrices, because the
stride has to count leaf elements, not immediate children. For

    dvec4 z[2][2][2][2];

the offset came out as 2a + 2b + 2c + d, which only ever addresses the first
eight of the sixteen elements and collides eight pairs; for

    dmat4 z[2][2][2][2];

it came out as 2a + 2b + 2c + d + column into a 64-element array. Both are
what the GL 4.6 arrays_of_arrays tests read back as wrong values on a driver
that translates through NIR to TGSI.

Take the stride from the number of leaf elements the dereferenced type still
contains - the product of its remaining array lengths times its matrix column
count - so the index matches how the split variables are laid out. dvec4
z[2][2][2][2] now addresses 8a + 4b + 2c + d over sixteen elements, and dmat4
z[2][2][2][2][col] addresses 32a + 16b + 8c + 4d + col over sixty-four. Types
the pass does not touch (scalars, dvec2, dmat2, and any 32-bit type) are
unaffected: their generated TGSI is byte-identical.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../nir/nir_split_64bit_vec3_and_vec4.c       | 19 ++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/compiler/nir/nir_split_64bit_vec3_and_vec4.c b/src/compiler/nir/nir_split_64bit_vec3_and_vec4.c
index 53900d265249dea43f6aef766b6ec45ae19e7ee0..c20dbeae8cb9fcaba63c1b205069c106aefdb11b 100644
--- a/src/compiler/nir/nir_split_64bit_vec3_and_vec4.c
+++ b/src/compiler/nir/nir_split_64bit_vec3_and_vec4.c
@@ -101,6 +101,18 @@ merge_to_vec3_or_vec4(nir_builder *b, nir_def *load1,
                       nir_channel(b, load2, 1));
 }
 
+/* The pair of split variables is a flat array holding one vector per leaf
+ * element of the original variable, so an index at any level has to step over
+ * every leaf element the type it dereferences still contains: the product of
+ * the remaining array lengths times the matrix column count.
+ */
+static unsigned
+leaf_element_count(const struct glsl_type *type)
+{
+   unsigned elements = glsl_type_is_array(type) ? glsl_get_aoa_size(type) : 1;
+   return elements * glsl_get_matrix_columns(glsl_without_array(type));
+}
+
 static nir_def *
 get_linear_array_offset(nir_builder *b, nir_deref_instr *deref)
 {
@@ -112,11 +124,8 @@ get_linear_array_offset(nir_builder *b, nir_deref_instr *deref)
       switch ((*p)->deref_type) {
       case nir_deref_type_array: {
          nir_def *index = (*p)->arr.index.ssa;
-         int stride = glsl_array_size((*p)->type);
-         if (stride >= 0)
-            offset = nir_iadd(b, offset, nir_amul_imm(b, index, stride));
-         else
-            offset = nir_iadd(b, offset, index);
+         unsigned stride = leaf_element_count((*p)->type);
+         offset = nir_iadd(b, offset, nir_amul_imm(b, index, stride));
          break;
       }
       default:
